1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
It's a half trillion dollar a year industry.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Gargantuan warehouses, enormous fleets of trucks,

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
hyperproductive processing plants,

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
climate controlled banana ripening rooms,

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
and computerized shopping carts.

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
All fueled by a variety of marketing strategies

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
designed to keep us coming back for more.

8
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, the supermarket on Modern Marble.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,640
They've changed our lives.

10
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:47,040
From the growth of self-service retailing

11
00:00:47,041 --> 00:00:49,900
to the availability of global foods.

12
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:54,080
From facilitating suburban sprawl

13
00:00:54,081 --> 00:00:57,040
to creating a greater reliance on the automobile.

14
00:00:57,041 --> 00:01:03,700
If jazz was the uniquely American contribution to music, the supermarket is the uniquely

15
00:01:03,701 --> 00:01:06,180
American contribution to shopping.

16
00:01:06,181 --> 00:01:11,540
It was invented here and spread very quickly around the world.

17
00:01:11,541 --> 00:01:16,260
Supermarkets are basically supersized grocery stores that are departmentalized and offer

18
00:01:16,261 --> 00:01:22,660
a wider selection of food, along with household merchandise and services.

19
00:01:22,661 --> 00:01:28,020
The traditional supermarket design occupies a large floor space on a single level, and

20
00:01:28,021 --> 00:01:34,540
usually has the convenience of an expansive parking lot and longer, if not around-the-clock

21
00:01:34,541 --> 00:01:37,220
shopping hours.

22
00:01:37,221 --> 00:01:43,040
Fiscally, they must bring in $2 million a year to qualify as a supermarket.

23
00:01:43,041 --> 00:01:46,160
Today we have about 34,000 supermarkets.

24
00:01:46,161 --> 00:01:50,300
They would do $480 billion in sales.

25
00:01:50,301 --> 00:01:56,320
The average supermarket would be a median size of 48,000 square feet, and they would

26
00:01:56,321 --> 00:02:03,920
carry about 45,000 different products in that store.

27
00:02:03,921 --> 00:02:08,880
A supermarket is often part of a chain that owns or controls other supermarkets located

28
00:02:08,881 --> 00:02:13,060
in the same town or multi-state region.

29
00:02:13,061 --> 00:02:17,900
This increases the opportunities to offer lower prices, made possible by volume sales

30
00:02:17,901 --> 00:02:19,520
on the wholesale and retail levels.

31
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:23,380
They've also had a big effect on food safety.

32
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:29,080
They can't afford to have 1,000 stores go out of business

33
00:02:29,081 --> 00:02:30,740
because something wasn't clean

34
00:02:30,741 --> 00:02:33,640
or they didn't follow sanitary conditions.

35
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:37,980
They really have one of the best food supplies in the world.

36
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:42,520
As shoppers travel up and down the aisles of their neighborhood store,

37
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:45,180
they are for the most part unaware

38
00:02:45,181 --> 00:02:49,660
of the massive behind-the-scenes operations it takes to keep a steady supply of everything,

39
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:54,880
from apples to Ziploc bags streaming to each of the stores in the chain.

40
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:01,760
Safeway is one of the largest food retailers in North America.

41
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:09,240
It operates over 1,700 stores in the Western and Mid-Atlantic regions of the United States

42
00:03:09,241 --> 00:03:11,080
and in Western Canada.

43
00:03:11,081 --> 00:03:18,960
To support its stores, Safeway has invested in extensive distribution and transportation systems.

44
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:25,220
To continually resupply our stores in Canada and the United States,

45
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:28,760
Safeway currently operates the 12th largest private fleet in North America,

46
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:31,900
with over 1,100 tractors and 4,500 trailers.

47
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:37,060
We travel approximately 85 million miles a year,

48
00:03:37,061 --> 00:03:42,060
deliver 475,000 truckloads of product to our retail stores every year.

49
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:51,460
Most of the fleet is outfitted with 64,000 BTU refrigeration units,

50
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:56,120
capable of keeping a trailer hauling frozen products at minus 20 degrees.

51
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:00,700
And the trucks are equipped with onboard computers.

52
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:06,940
The onboard computer tells the driver exactly where he is to go

53
00:04:06,941 --> 00:04:10,880
what store he is to go to, how many pallets of product he is to deliver at each store.

54
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:16,440
The onboard computer also provides the driver with a method to track DOT hours of service,

55
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:19,200
which is required currently by the Department of Transportation.

56
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:29,440
To service its truck fleet, some of Safeway's distribution centers have their own maintenance areas,

57
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:31,640
fueling stations, and automated truck washes.

58
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:50,340
Safeway operates 13 distribution warehouse centers in the United States and four in Canada.

59
00:04:50,341 --> 00:04:55,260
One of the company's newest centers, located in Auburn, Washington, services its stores

60
00:04:55,261 --> 00:04:59,780
in the northwestern United States.

61
00:04:59,781 --> 00:05:12,660
The sprawling 116-acre complex includes administrative offices, a recycling center, and two enormous

62
00:05:12,661 --> 00:05:17,160
warehouses.

63
00:05:17,161 --> 00:05:22,780
Surrounding the buildings is just over 2 million square feet of 9-inch-thick concrete paving

64
00:05:22,781 --> 00:05:29,780
to provide a durable surface to handle the heavy truck traffic.

65
00:05:29,781 --> 00:05:35,240
The largest facility is the grocery warehouse.

66
00:05:35,241 --> 00:05:40,400
Inside are nearly three miles of shelving filled with product ready for shipment.

67
00:05:40,401 --> 00:05:48,120
This particular building is 518,000 square feet, holds about 44,000 pallet positions,

68
00:05:48,121 --> 00:05:52,160
typically will ship in the neighborhood of around 2 million pounds a day.

69
00:05:52,580 --> 00:05:56,780
We typically have two shifts that are picking products and shipping to our stores.

70
00:05:56,781 --> 00:06:00,340
We deliver on a 24-hour basis, 7 days a week.

71
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:06,760
An army of forklifts load incoming pallets on the shelves

72
00:06:06,761 --> 00:06:09,660
and make sure the ground level has product available

73
00:06:09,661 --> 00:06:13,640
for those who are putting together the outgoing orders to be delivered to stores.

74
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:17,940
Those responsible for order fulfillment are called pickers.

75
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:25,000
The headsets the pickers wear are part of an interactive computerized system designed

76
00:06:25,001 --> 00:06:36,240
to make assembling an order more accurate.

77
00:06:36,241 --> 00:06:41,560
The voice selection technology is a technology that has taken the place of the old paper

78
00:06:41,561 --> 00:06:45,760
list of the things to pick on a pallet to go to a store.

79
00:06:45,761 --> 00:06:49,660
delivers the list of things via the headset to the order picker.

80
00:06:49,661 --> 00:06:55,180
The computer just told me to go to slot 444 of aisle 5.

81
00:06:58,000 --> 00:06:59,600
So I pull up to the slot.

82
00:07:01,580 --> 00:07:03,360
Slot location 444.

83
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:06,040
Confirmation number 44.

84
00:07:07,400 --> 00:07:09,760
The computer tells me to pick one item.

85
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:11,760
So I pick up one item.

86
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:15,400
And I tell the computer, one.

87
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:18,640
451.

88
00:07:18,980 --> 00:07:23,180
McPeter then tells me to go to the next location.

89
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:34,280
The building that serves as the cold storage and perishable warehouse is one of the largest in the nation.

90
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:41,640
The powerful refrigeration equipment keeps the different rooms at specific temperatures.

91
00:07:45,760 --> 00:07:51,760
The coldest is the frozen foods warehouse that is maintained at 15 degrees below zero.

92
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:55,260
Produce is kept in the low 50s.

93
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:03,100
Bananas are picked and shipped green and need some special handling.

94
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:07,040
So Safeway built 12 banana ripening rooms in the warehouse.

95
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:13,300
Each one can hold up to 1,040-pound boxes of bananas.

96
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:23,780
By controlling the temperature and humidity in each room, Safeway can speed up or slow down the ripening process to keep pace with consumer demand.

97
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:32,820
We're essentially trapping the gases the same way you would trap the gases in a paper bag with any type of fruit and facilitate the ripening process.

98
00:08:33,420 --> 00:08:43,660
This technique eliminates the need for unboxing and re-boxing this easily bruised fruit, resulting in significant savings of time, space, and labor costs.

99
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:54,760
Among the produce and groceries distributed to the stores of major supermarket chains are a selection of products they manufacture themselves.

100
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:02,760
Safeway's bakeries get an early start to mass produce a fresh supply of bread.

101
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:08,760
At the facility in Bellevue, Washington, white flour is brought in by the train car load.

102
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:12,760
Presently, we are in our bulk storage area.

103
00:09:12,761 --> 00:09:17,010
area. This tank here holds 110,000 pounds of flour.

104
00:09:17,011 --> 00:09:19,760
From this tank, we blow the flour

105
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:22,046
into a sifter.

106
00:09:22,047 --> 00:09:27,920
From this sifter, the flour goes to the mixer, where we use it for making

107
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:33,600
bread.

108
00:09:33,601 --> 00:09:40,420
Once the flour is mixed with the other ingredients, the dough is cut into appropriate sized pieces,

109
00:09:40,421 --> 00:09:46,020
shaped and put into baking pans.

110
00:09:46,021 --> 00:09:49,520
Then it travels to the proof box so the dough can rise.

111
00:09:49,521 --> 00:09:57,040
Once the bread comes out of the proof box, we go on this conveyor into the oven where

112
00:09:57,041 --> 00:09:58,040
it's baked.

113
00:09:58,041 --> 00:10:07,500
In the oven, it can take anywhere from 19 minutes to 24 minutes, depending on the variety.

114
00:10:07,501 --> 00:10:12,500
After the bread is baked, it's cooled for 60 minutes.

115
00:10:12,501 --> 00:10:18,500
After the bread is cooled, now we are slicing it here.

116
00:10:18,501 --> 00:10:22,500
After the bread is sliced, we put it in a bag.

117
00:10:22,501 --> 00:10:25,500
The bag is open with air.

118
00:10:25,501 --> 00:10:32,500
After the bread is in the bag, we enclose it, as you can see, with a little quick lock.

119
00:10:32,501 --> 00:10:38,460
The whole bread process takes about three hours, and right now we are producing about

120
00:10:38,461 --> 00:10:40,340
6,000 loaves an hour.

121
00:10:40,341 --> 00:10:45,900
And that's the bread business.

122
00:10:45,901 --> 00:10:52,320
Another line of product many chains package under their own brands is dairy.

123
00:10:52,321 --> 00:10:57,200
At Safeway's Bellevue milk plant, truck after truck arrives filled with milk purchased from

124
00:10:57,201 --> 00:10:59,320
local dairy farms.

125
00:10:59,321 --> 00:11:04,820
We're looking at a scene where we're pumping 7,000 gallons of milk into these silos behind us.

126
00:11:04,821 --> 00:11:08,140
These storage silos hold upwards to 60,000 gallons.

127
00:11:08,141 --> 00:11:12,560
We're looking at a storage capacity of a quarter million gallons of milk for the day.

128
00:11:12,561 --> 00:11:18,720
Inside, the first step is to pasteurize the raw milk by raising its temperature until

129
00:11:18,721 --> 00:11:23,120
harmful bacteria and other unwanted organisms are killed.

130
00:11:23,121 --> 00:11:26,620
it's processed into a variety of milks, whips, and half and half.

131
00:11:29,620 --> 00:11:34,680
Since the plant outputs so many gallons of milk a year, Safeway decided to install an

132
00:11:34,681 --> 00:11:40,300
assembly line to blow mold plastic gallon jugs on site at a rate of over two million per month.

133
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:50,060
Once they're trimmed, they travel across the building to be filled with milk.

134
00:11:53,120 --> 00:12:02,160
The Bellevue plant also produces 20 million pounds of cottage cheese a year.

135
00:12:02,161 --> 00:12:06,700
The process to make Lucerne Cottage Cheese is to fill our vats which holds 50,000 pounds

136
00:12:06,701 --> 00:12:13,020
of skim milk, heat the product up, add bacteria, and separate the whey from the curd.

137
00:12:13,021 --> 00:12:17,440
From there we pump the curd up to mixers and we make our 4% all the way down to our fat-free

138
00:12:17,441 --> 00:12:19,000
Lucerne Cottage Cheese.

139
00:12:19,001 --> 00:12:23,060
From there, after it's mixed, it goes into the packaging to cold storage and outdoor

140
00:12:23,061 --> 00:12:29,360
distribution channels.

141
00:12:29,361 --> 00:12:33,180
In an adjacent building is an ice cream plant.

142
00:12:33,181 --> 00:12:35,760
It's one of the largest in the United States.

143
00:12:35,761 --> 00:12:41,960
There are two assembly lines to fill gallon containers, one designed to handle round cartons

144
00:12:41,961 --> 00:12:49,760
and the other to fill square ones.

145
00:12:49,761 --> 00:12:54,800
Both of the lines output one gallon of ice cream per second, adding up to a total of

146
00:12:54,801 --> 00:13:00,560
18 million gallons per year.

147
00:13:00,561 --> 00:13:05,560
The facility also manufactures a variety of ice cream treats.

148
00:13:05,561 --> 00:13:12,920
The new machine puts together ice cream sandwiches at the rate of 1,800 per hour.

149
00:13:12,921 --> 00:13:18,500
This assembly line produces 350 stick bars per minute.

150
00:13:18,501 --> 00:13:25,620
Safeway manufactures between 40 and 50 million bars per year.

151
00:13:25,621 --> 00:13:31,200
In addition to the items supermarket chains produce themselves and other domestic products,

152
00:13:31,201 --> 00:13:36,340
Foods from around the world are a common part of the vast selection.

153
00:13:36,341 --> 00:13:41,680
Fresh produce on display in stores may have been picked on another continent.

154
00:13:41,681 --> 00:13:48,920
And cheeses from France are sold alongside those from a local dairy.

155
00:13:48,921 --> 00:13:53,380
The equipment the entire grocery industry needs to warehouse, transport and display

156
00:13:53,381 --> 00:13:58,072
perishable and frozen foods accounts for about one-third of the total U.S.

157
00:13:58,073 --> 00:13:58,960
energy use for

158
00:13:58,961 --> 00:14:03,500
refrigeration.

159
00:14:03,501 --> 00:14:08,520
This gigantic enterprise had to start somewhere.

160
00:14:08,521 --> 00:14:13,720
And a big king and a little pig did their part to make supermarkets the nation's supreme

161
00:14:13,721 --> 00:14:18,080
suppliers of food.

162
00:14:18,081 --> 00:14:22,740
According to the Food Marketing Institute, the supermarket industry's workforce of union

163
00:14:22,741 --> 00:14:27,520
and non-union personnel is almost three and a half million strong.

164
00:14:27,521 --> 00:14:30,980
The supermarket will return on Modern Marvels.

165
00:14:31,640 --> 00:14:35,260
We now return to the supermarket on Modern Marvels.

166
00:14:38,080 --> 00:14:42,600
Supermarkets may have played a vital role in the development of our modern suburban lifestyle.

167
00:14:44,560 --> 00:14:50,460
But providing an adequate food supply has been an essential element in the success of cultures throughout human history.

168
00:14:52,100 --> 00:14:54,560
We can't get along without it.

169
00:14:54,561 --> 00:14:56,740
We can't transport without it.

170
00:14:56,741 --> 00:14:59,580
Napoleon says an army moves on its belly.

171
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:05,740
So this has been a key ingredient to civilization and society.

172
00:15:07,580 --> 00:15:12,500
Wherever major cities developed, street vendors, stores, and markets flourished

173
00:15:12,501 --> 00:15:17,440
to supply the urban population so far removed from the self-sufficiency of farm life.

174
00:15:19,940 --> 00:15:22,960
During the settlement of North America by Europeans,

175
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:26,380
trading posts were some of the first retail establishments.

176
00:15:26,740 --> 00:15:30,220
they made available foods that people couldn't grow or hunt themselves,

177
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:34,240
including sugar and coffee, plus basic supplies like blankets.

178
00:15:36,020 --> 00:15:38,160
As more people settled into an area,

179
00:15:38,820 --> 00:15:41,900
larger retail businesses known as general stores appeared.

180
00:15:43,800 --> 00:15:47,060
They sold a wider variety of foods and canned goods,

181
00:15:48,500 --> 00:15:51,540
along with just about everything else for the home or farm.

182
00:15:51,541 --> 00:15:59,780
As urban populations grew, stores became more specialized, and usually neighborhoods had

183
00:15:59,781 --> 00:16:05,360
their own butcher shop, bakery, and grocery store.

184
00:16:05,361 --> 00:16:09,940
By the beginning of the 20th century, the Great Atlantic and Pacific Tea Company, or

185
00:16:09,941 --> 00:16:15,160
A&P, had become America's first major chain of grocery stores.

186
00:16:15,161 --> 00:16:20,780
Shopping in a store 100 years ago was very different than it is today.

187
00:16:20,781 --> 00:16:23,180
These were set up with clerks who did all the work.

188
00:16:23,320 --> 00:16:28,660
All a customer had to do was walk in the store, hand the clerk a list of items that were to be purchased.

189
00:16:28,980 --> 00:16:31,020
The customer's not allowed behind the counters.

190
00:16:31,460 --> 00:16:34,240
And the clerk would select the items for the customer.

191
00:16:35,240 --> 00:16:40,420
In the early 20th century, Clarence Saunders, a grocery store owner in Memphis, Tennessee,

192
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:44,580
felt that having clerks gather up each customer's order was too labor-intensive.

193
00:16:44,581 --> 00:16:53,220
Today at the Pink Palace Museum in Memphis is an exact replica of his creation that revolutionized

194
00:16:53,221 --> 00:16:55,560
the grocery industry.

195
00:16:55,561 --> 00:16:58,960
The first successful self-service grocery store.

196
00:16:58,961 --> 00:17:04,000
No one seems to know why he named his store Piggly Wiggly, but customers flooded in when

197
00:17:04,001 --> 00:17:06,640
it opened in 1916.

198
00:17:06,641 --> 00:17:10,260
And there were innovations that customers would see that would really influence their

199
00:17:10,261 --> 00:17:11,860
shopping habits.

200
00:17:11,861 --> 00:17:14,620
The first is the turnstile.

201
00:17:14,621 --> 00:17:19,220
Once you went into the store, you couldn't get out until you finished your entire trip

202
00:17:19,221 --> 00:17:20,880
to the store.

203
00:17:20,881 --> 00:17:23,440
The second was a shopping basket.

204
00:17:23,441 --> 00:17:27,820
Shopping baskets allowed the customer to pick up the produce and the materials and to put

205
00:17:27,821 --> 00:17:29,100
them in the basket themselves.

206
00:17:29,101 --> 00:17:34,080
One of the most important ideas of Clarence Saunders was the use of price tags.

207
00:17:34,081 --> 00:17:38,020
For the first time, customers knew exactly how much their products cost.

208
00:17:38,021 --> 00:17:41,960
They could select the quality of the goods that they wanted to buy, put them in their

209
00:17:41,961 --> 00:17:46,200
basket and go through the store and continue shopping.

210
00:17:46,201 --> 00:17:50,560
Piggly Wiggly stores multiplied as other grocery store chains adopted the self-service

211
00:17:50,561 --> 00:17:53,300
format.

212
00:17:53,301 --> 00:18:02,360
But it would be more than a decade before the larger supermarket format made its debut.

213
00:18:02,361 --> 00:18:07,860
Today on New York's Long Island, there are 45 stores of a family-owned chain of supermarkets

214
00:18:07,861 --> 00:18:10,800
named King Cullen.

215
00:18:10,801 --> 00:18:15,020
According to the Smithsonian Institution, these are the direct descendants of the first

216
00:18:15,021 --> 00:18:21,160
true supermarket that opened in 1930.

217
00:18:21,161 --> 00:18:22,960
It was the brainchild of Michael J.

218
00:18:22,961 --> 00:18:25,480
Cullen, who had first come up with the idea while

219
00:18:25,481 --> 00:18:29,360
working for A&P and Kroger.

220
00:18:29,361 --> 00:18:34,240
In a letter to his employers, he outlined the concept of a bigger store, in which lower

221
00:18:34,241 --> 00:18:38,900
prices would be offset by a higher volume of sales.

222
00:18:38,901 --> 00:18:46,400
Michael Cullen, my uncle, sent this famous letter outlining his vision of a supermarket.

223
00:18:46,401 --> 00:18:50,300
And of course they didn't do anything with it.

224
00:18:50,301 --> 00:18:56,720
So Mike moved to New York and teamed up with a wholesaler who gave him an advance to start

225
00:18:56,721 --> 00:19:00,780
for a supermarket, which he did in Jamaica, Long Island, in 1930.

226
00:19:01,740 --> 00:19:03,420
His timing was perfect.

227
00:19:03,900 --> 00:19:06,260
By 1930, most households had an automobile.

228
00:19:08,560 --> 00:19:10,880
Refrigerators were becoming a standard home appliance.

229
00:19:12,540 --> 00:19:16,080
And America was sinking deeper and deeper into the Great Depression.

230
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:21,100
Lower prices were a welcome relief.

231
00:19:22,420 --> 00:19:25,960
Mike was a great promoter, and his two big slogans that he used,

232
00:19:25,961 --> 00:19:29,160
and he used them quite effectively, put them on his stores and his ads.

233
00:19:29,500 --> 00:19:31,460
One was the world's greatest price wrecker,

234
00:19:31,780 --> 00:19:34,060
and the other is, why pay more?

235
00:19:34,500 --> 00:19:37,000
And he pounded those home consistently.

236
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:42,060
By 1936, there were 17 King Cullen supermarkets

237
00:19:42,061 --> 00:19:45,280
doing approximately $6 million in business annually.

238
00:19:46,820 --> 00:19:49,200
Retailers who had held off on adopting the concept,

239
00:19:49,740 --> 00:19:50,620
thinking it might fail,

240
00:19:51,280 --> 00:19:53,840
finally realized that supermarkets were the future.

241
00:19:55,960 --> 00:20:02,960
America's entry into World War II widened the supermarket's place in the food retailing industry.

242
00:20:02,961 --> 00:20:09,960
During the first three years of the war, thousands of grocery stores, which were usually small, family-run businesses,

243
00:20:09,961 --> 00:20:16,960
had to close their doors when the head of the household went off to join the armed services or work in the defense industry.

244
00:20:16,961 --> 00:20:17,732
industry.

245
00:20:17,733 --> 00:20:24,420
At the end of the war, supermarkets were in a perfect position to benefit from

246
00:20:24,421 --> 00:20:29,828
the period of unprecedented prosperity and growth that followed.

247
00:20:29,829 --> 00:20:31,940
In the 1950s, they became

248
00:20:31,941 --> 00:20:34,765
symbolic of the American way of life.

249
00:20:34,766 --> 00:20:38,660
In the late 1950s, leaders from all over the world,

250
00:20:38,661 --> 00:20:44,520
Queen Elizabeth specifically and Nikita Khrushchev specifically visiting America, and the thing

251
00:20:44,521 --> 00:20:48,840
they all wanted to see when they were here was a typical American supermarket.

252
00:20:48,841 --> 00:20:54,080
It was a symbol of the benefits of our economic system to the average citizen,

253
00:20:54,280 --> 00:20:56,360
day in and day out, week in and week out.

254
00:20:58,240 --> 00:21:04,680
By the 1960s, supermarkets were responsible for more than 70% of American grocery sales.

255
00:21:05,840 --> 00:21:09,140
Then in the 1980s, warehouse stores began to appear,

256
00:21:09,440 --> 00:21:11,880
proving people were willing to buy in bulk size

257
00:21:11,881 --> 00:21:14,600
in a more industrial-looking environment to save money.

258
00:21:16,900 --> 00:21:19,780
Supermarket owners converted some of their stores to this format.

259
00:21:22,840 --> 00:21:24,800
Supermarkets have continually restructured

260
00:21:24,801 --> 00:21:26,960
to keep up with changing consumer desires.

261
00:21:28,240 --> 00:21:30,740
In reaction to modern life's busy schedules,

262
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:33,980
prepared meals departments are becoming increasingly important

263
00:21:33,981 --> 00:21:36,120
and being given more floor space.

264
00:21:36,121 --> 00:21:43,680
Many supermarkets are targeting particular groups of customers, like those interested

265
00:21:43,681 --> 00:21:51,180
in organic foods or specific ethnic groups or immigrant populations.

266
00:21:51,181 --> 00:21:56,720
Over 30% of all new supermarkets under construction in the United States are aimed at Hispanic

267
00:21:56,721 --> 00:21:57,720
customers.

268
00:21:57,721 --> 00:22:04,720
But the evolution of supermarkets has always reflected societal change.

269
00:22:04,721 --> 00:22:10,720
And that evolution has always been facilitated by new technologies.

270
00:22:10,721 --> 00:22:15,720
When in a supermarket, we're always one step closer to the future.

271
00:22:15,721 --> 00:22:21,720
When the first Piggly Wiggly opened for business on September 6, 1916,

272
00:22:21,721 --> 00:22:24,720
its shelves were stocked with just over 600 different items.

273
00:22:24,721 --> 00:22:30,020
The average checkout total was 96 cents, but a bottle of ketchup cost only 8 cents.

274
00:22:31,320 --> 00:22:34,160
The supermarket will return on Modern Marvels.

275
00:22:35,640 --> 00:22:39,380
We now return to the supermarket on Modern Marvels.

276
00:22:42,820 --> 00:22:48,060
Owner's attempts to make customers' visits to supermarkets as accommodating and efficient as possible

277
00:22:48,061 --> 00:22:51,180
have led to many important innovations over the years.

278
00:22:51,181 --> 00:22:56,180
Some have been so successful they spread to other retail applications.

279
00:22:56,181 --> 00:23:07,180
Perhaps the most widely used and copied is the standardized barcode, known as the Universal Product Code, or UPC.

280
00:23:07,181 --> 00:23:14,180
Each product is assigned a number, which is represented by a set of lines and spaces of varying thicknesses.

281
00:23:14,181 --> 00:23:18,180
Scanners using a laser or digital camera are used to read the codes.

282
00:23:21,180 --> 00:23:26,180
Supermarkets were the first to use the system at the retail point of sale.

283
00:23:26,181 --> 00:23:34,180
IBM's first systems that were able to process barcodes were the IBM 3660 supermarket system,

284
00:23:34,181 --> 00:23:39,180
which consisted of a controller, registers, and scanners.

285
00:23:39,181 --> 00:23:45,180
And these systems were first shipped in July, August of 1974.

286
00:23:45,181 --> 00:23:50,180
The use of barcodes revolutionized both the checkout process and inventory management.

287
00:23:50,181 --> 00:23:58,260
As the system checks you out, it's not only calculating your prices, but it's also writing

288
00:23:58,261 --> 00:24:02,640
down every item that goes out of the store so at the end of the day the retailer knows

289
00:24:02,641 --> 00:24:06,260
every item that went out so that he can reorder automatically.

290
00:24:06,261 --> 00:24:10,940
What may be the most important shopping aid ever invented, the shopping cart, was created

291
00:24:10,941 --> 00:24:18,740
back in 1937 by Sylvan Goldman, a supermarket owner in Oklahoma City.

292
00:24:18,741 --> 00:24:22,300
He had noticed shoppers struggling with the metal shopping baskets they carried through

293
00:24:22,301 --> 00:24:23,300
the store.

294
00:24:23,301 --> 00:24:27,360
Then, a folding chair in his office gave him an idea.

295
00:24:27,361 --> 00:24:31,240
It could be mounted on wheels, and the seat could serve as a platform for the shopping

296
00:24:31,241 --> 00:24:33,340
basket.

297
00:24:33,341 --> 00:24:38,600
After some more tinkering on prototypes, Goldman decided to provide more storage space by having

298
00:24:38,601 --> 00:24:41,920
double-decker baskets.

299
00:24:41,921 --> 00:24:47,100
Once he manufactured his carts, he still had to convince the public to use them.

300
00:24:47,101 --> 00:24:50,471
walking into the stores would tend to say one of two things.

301
00:24:50,472 --> 00:24:52,100
If it was a woman, she'd say,

302
00:24:52,180 --> 00:24:55,889
I've been pushing a baby carriage so long, I don't want to push another cart.

303
00:24:55,890 --> 00:24:56,660
If it was a man,

304
00:24:56,740 --> 00:25:00,880
he'd say, don't I look muscular enough to carry one of these little baskets around?

305
00:25:01,560 --> 00:25:07,200
So Goldman actually had to hire shoppers around the front of the store to push these carts with

306
00:25:07,201 --> 00:25:07,901
products in them.

307
00:25:07,902 --> 00:25:10,880
And then a lady at the front of the store when somebody came in would say,

308
00:25:10,881 --> 00:25:13,840
well, hey, look, everybody's using the cart. Why not you?

309
00:25:13,841 --> 00:25:19,580
It would be another 10 years before the typical shopping cart we know today appeared.

310
00:25:20,820 --> 00:25:23,600
A larger single basket replaced the double-decker basket.

311
00:25:24,540 --> 00:25:29,700
And by redesigning it so that the back end would swing, one cart could be pushed into another.

312
00:25:30,920 --> 00:25:38,080
A supermarket could have hundreds of them in a very small area, not take up space that could be used to sell products.

313
00:25:38,081 --> 00:25:39,991
But it did more than that.

314
00:25:39,992 --> 00:25:44,620
It made it easier for parents to bring their children shopping.

315
00:25:45,180 --> 00:25:48,960
Not only that, it provided them a space to put heavier items.

316
00:25:49,700 --> 00:26:00,260
Not only that, it provided advertisers a place to remind consumers every second that they're in the store of their products.

317
00:26:01,360 --> 00:26:04,880
Today's low-tech shopping cart is getting some high-tech additions.

318
00:26:08,080 --> 00:26:15,180
Where shopping cart theft is particularly frequent, some stores have invested in electronic locking wheels.

319
00:26:17,420 --> 00:26:27,140
The wheel locks are triggered when they pass over a wire, emitting an electronic signal that is buried just under the cement or asphalt along the store's property line.

320
00:26:29,600 --> 00:26:37,500
And the IBM Industries Solutions Lab in Hawthorne, New York, has been working on a wireless touchscreen computer to smarten up the carts.

321
00:26:38,080 --> 00:26:44,060
When you walk into the supermarket, you take this out of the charging rack and you place it on your cart.

322
00:26:46,960 --> 00:26:54,760
Then what you do is you take the scanner, you scan your loyalty card so it knows who you are.

323
00:26:55,560 --> 00:27:01,120
I then hit start shopping and I begin my shopping trip.

324
00:27:01,121 --> 00:27:09,460
As I go through the store, I'm able to scan my items and bag it as I go.

325
00:27:11,600 --> 00:27:16,740
The ability to scan selections and keep a running total is only one of the computer's functions.

326
00:27:18,380 --> 00:27:23,920
Customers can enter their shopping list via the Internet, and the computer will tell them where the items are in the store.

327
00:27:25,640 --> 00:27:28,220
It also lets you know when your deli order is ready.

328
00:27:28,221 --> 00:27:31,540
and it offers recipes and personalized coupons.

329
00:27:33,680 --> 00:27:36,700
And because the customers scan and bag as they shop,

330
00:27:37,340 --> 00:27:40,320
checkout only involves payment of the already established total.

331
00:27:44,400 --> 00:27:48,160
Another innovation IBM has created is VeggieVision.

332
00:27:49,220 --> 00:27:52,400
It takes the guesswork out of pricing fruits and vegetables

333
00:27:52,401 --> 00:27:57,360
by using a digital camera to capture an image of the object placed on the scale.

334
00:27:58,220 --> 00:28:01,380
so it can be visually compared to a database of stored imagery.

335
00:28:01,381 --> 00:28:08,720
What VeggieVision does is it has a visual recognition system that looks at the hue,

336
00:28:08,721 --> 00:28:11,460
the saturation and the intensity.

337
00:28:11,461 --> 00:28:16,580
It's looking at the segmentation, so the lightness or darkness of that fruit or vegetable.

338
00:28:16,581 --> 00:28:20,200
And finally the features, the texture, curvature and width.

339
00:28:20,201 --> 00:28:24,900
It's making an analysis and recognizing that it's a tomato.

340
00:28:24,901 --> 00:28:30,480
So now all you have to do is print out a barcode tag and you're ready to leave the store.

341
00:28:30,481 --> 00:28:36,160
As the number of self-checkout lines increases, technologies like Vigivision may become an

342
00:28:36,161 --> 00:28:41,340
important part of supermarket modernization.

343
00:28:41,341 --> 00:28:47,720
But there's another technology under development that could do away with checkout lines altogether.

344
00:28:47,721 --> 00:28:50,300
This is the future of item tagging.

345
00:28:50,301 --> 00:28:54,380
This is an RFID tag, radio frequency identification tag.

346
00:28:54,381 --> 00:28:57,500
They consist of an antenna and a microprocessor.

347
00:28:57,501 --> 00:29:03,840
It essentially carries the same information and more as a barcode does today.

348
00:29:03,841 --> 00:29:09,260
When these tags become cost effective and all items are tagged with RFID tags, when

349
00:29:09,261 --> 00:29:13,500
you go shopping your checkout operation will be much faster.

350
00:29:13,501 --> 00:29:18,180
You will gather your items, pass them across a scanner, or walk through a portal and your

351
00:29:18,181 --> 00:29:21,720
transaction is done.

352
00:29:21,721 --> 00:29:26,720
Once RFID technology is perfected for use at checkout, it will be possible for a customer

353
00:29:26,721 --> 00:29:29,080
to simply leave the store.

354
00:29:29,081 --> 00:29:32,460
All of the tags on the items in the cart will be read, along with the customer's credit

355
00:29:32,461 --> 00:29:36,160
card, which will have RFID technology as well.

356
00:29:36,161 --> 00:29:41,280
The total will be charged directly to their account.

357
00:29:41,281 --> 00:29:46,640
Already in place is another technology that makes it possible never to set foot in a supermarket.

358
00:29:46,641 --> 00:29:48,960
The Internet.

359
00:29:48,961 --> 00:29:53,460
New York City, with its concentrated population, was the perfect proving ground for an internet

360
00:29:53,461 --> 00:29:57,520
grocery company called Fresh Direct.

361
00:29:57,521 --> 00:29:58,640
Here's how it works.

362
00:29:58,641 --> 00:30:03,120
The customer places their order online at the Fresh Direct website.

363
00:30:03,121 --> 00:30:09,040
When all the orders are done being placed today, we begin picking the produce and getting

364
00:30:09,041 --> 00:30:14,800
all of the other items that our customers have ordered organized in boxes in our 300,000

365
00:30:14,801 --> 00:30:17,560
square foot facility.

366
00:30:17,561 --> 00:30:22,480
There are miles of conveyor belt and at the end of the process there is 130 refrigerated

367
00:30:22,481 --> 00:30:31,560
trucks that deliver the product into the city and into the suburbs.

368
00:30:31,561 --> 00:30:36,400
Supermarkets have always relied on technological innovations.

369
00:30:36,401 --> 00:30:45,480
But maximizing sales also depends on employing some of the social sciences, especially psychology.

370
00:30:45,481 --> 00:30:52,720
The first item barcode scanned in a supermarket checkout line was a pack of chewing gum on June 26, 1974.

371
00:30:53,920 --> 00:30:56,960
It's now on display in the Smithsonian Institution.

372
00:30:58,140 --> 00:31:01,020
The supermarket will return on Modern Marvels.

373
00:31:04,200 --> 00:31:07,820
We now return to the supermarket on Modern Marvels.

374
00:31:07,821 --> 00:31:15,440
Supermarket owners give much thought to how to get people into their stores and encourage

375
00:31:15,441 --> 00:31:20,400
them to become loyal customers.

376
00:31:20,401 --> 00:31:25,020
Location is obviously a primary factor and so is the promise of low prices.

377
00:31:25,021 --> 00:31:31,540
There are two basic pricing strategies supermarkets typically use to convince customers they're

378
00:31:31,541 --> 00:31:35,860
getting the most out of their food dollar.

379
00:31:35,861 --> 00:31:39,420
One they call everyday low pricing, EDLP.

380
00:31:39,421 --> 00:31:44,980
EDLP is really a model that says let's try and find what the lowest possible price is.

381
00:31:44,981 --> 00:31:47,180
Let's sell it that way all the time.

382
00:31:47,181 --> 00:31:49,480
The other is called high-low.

383
00:31:49,481 --> 00:31:57,120
High-low is where they really try and put some products on promotion that are just incredible

384
00:31:57,121 --> 00:32:02,320
values and they use these to bring customers in who buy the full basket of products which

385
00:32:02,321 --> 00:32:04,460
include products at their regular levels.

386
00:32:05,860 --> 00:32:13,240
Beyond pricing strategies, supermarket owners have done a great deal of analysis in determining how the layout of the store impacts sales.

387
00:32:16,220 --> 00:32:24,587
First impressions are important, and stores want to appear clean and filled with fresh foods, so owners often place produce sections at the entrance of their

388
00:32:24,588 --> 00:32:25,288
supermarkets.

389
00:32:25,289 --> 00:32:32,180
Many stores have installed tungsten rather than fluorescent lights over the produce department

390
00:32:32,181 --> 00:32:36,240
because it helps make the fruits and vegetables look brighter and more colorful.

391
00:32:38,680 --> 00:32:44,460
Almost all supermarkets place the frozen foods in the aisles nearest the checkout to minimize thawing.

392
00:32:45,420 --> 00:32:49,820
But there are several reasons why most supermarkets have the perishables around the perimeter

393
00:32:49,821 --> 00:32:54,040
and the dry goods and household items on the shelves in the center of the store.

394
00:32:55,280 --> 00:33:01,400
Refrigerated cases need to be where they can get to the equipment that runs those cases,

395
00:33:01,401 --> 00:33:05,120
the dairy case or the frozen cases or the meat cutting room.

396
00:33:05,121 --> 00:33:07,200
So part of it's practical.

397
00:33:07,201 --> 00:33:10,040
But this arrangement is also how shoppers prefer it.

398
00:33:10,041 --> 00:33:15,800
In 1964, Department of Agriculture Studies found that if the perishable items were placed

399
00:33:15,801 --> 00:33:21,340
in the center aisles instead of around the periphery of the store, there was a decline

400
00:33:21,341 --> 00:33:23,720
in the number of items purchased.

401
00:33:23,721 --> 00:33:30,720
One reason why commonly purchased products like milk are placed in the back of the store

402
00:33:30,721 --> 00:33:33,720
has to do with marketing strategies to maximize sales.

403
00:33:33,721 --> 00:33:39,720
The idea that a supermarket would have in strategically thinking about what its layout is,

404
00:33:39,721 --> 00:33:45,720
is to make sure that the consumer sees as much of the product as possible.

405
00:33:45,721 --> 00:33:49,720
And so there's a couple things that you would want to put far away from the door.

406
00:33:49,721 --> 00:33:52,720
And milk is usually the example given.

407
00:33:52,721 --> 00:33:58,620
There are many marketing strategies that dictate exactly where products are placed on store shelves.

408
00:33:59,620 --> 00:34:02,660
There have been a lot of studies done on shelf placement,

409
00:34:02,940 --> 00:34:09,660
and it is obvious that eye level, female eye level, I think, is about 54 inches or something like that,

410
00:34:09,880 --> 00:34:12,980
and male eye level is a little higher, is the premium place.

411
00:34:13,060 --> 00:34:16,400
So vertical placement on the shelf makes a huge difference.

412
00:34:16,800 --> 00:34:19,040
And marketers who are marketing to children,

413
00:34:19,140 --> 00:34:22,220
because children can be very influential decision makers in the supermarket,

414
00:34:22,221 --> 00:34:27,020
will tend to put items that might appeal to children, sugared cereals, candy,

415
00:34:27,360 --> 00:34:30,120
at the kind of level that a kid might see.

416
00:34:30,680 --> 00:34:35,180
It certainly makes sense that you put the best products at eye level,

417
00:34:36,000 --> 00:34:40,820
the most famous brands want to be at eye level, but there's a better spot.

418
00:34:41,320 --> 00:34:43,620
The better spot is right at the end of the aisle.

419
00:34:43,740 --> 00:34:45,480
In the industry, we call them end caps.

420
00:34:45,880 --> 00:34:48,480
As they say, pile it high and watch it fly.

421
00:34:48,481 --> 00:34:54,380
One of the things that they know is that you're going to spend some time at the checkout counter

422
00:34:54,381 --> 00:34:58,400
and looking at items, you know, to entertain yourself while you're sitting there waiting.

423
00:34:58,820 --> 00:35:02,820
And so they tend to put impulse-type items right at the checkout counter.

424
00:35:06,140 --> 00:35:09,340
Supermarkets also pay a great deal of attention to atmospherics,

425
00:35:09,660 --> 00:35:14,300
the things that affect customers' visual, auditory, and even olfactory sensations.

426
00:35:14,301 --> 00:35:20,920
We're seeing all the major supermarkets with fresh bakeries, and it has two advantages.

427
00:35:21,560 --> 00:35:24,460
Number one is it's an opportunity to sell product.

428
00:35:24,860 --> 00:35:30,320
Number two, it's an opportunity to send that bakery aroma throughout the store.

429
00:35:30,920 --> 00:35:38,560
And so when people walk in saying, I'm not that hungry, they get in the front door and they say, I am hungry.

430
00:35:39,080 --> 00:35:42,860
Which translates into, what can I buy now?

431
00:35:44,300 --> 00:35:54,640
The evidence on music shows that soothing music, slow tempo music, makes people stay in the supermarket longer, and that makes a difference.

432
00:35:54,641 --> 00:35:57,920
Softer lighting will make people stay in the supermarket longer.

433
00:35:58,240 --> 00:36:01,540
Harsh lighting tends to make people just want to rush and get out of there.

434
00:36:02,060 --> 00:36:03,540
There's also uses of colors.

435
00:36:04,120 --> 00:36:13,260
There's a general sense that blues, for example, are more pleasant, and people like to stay around and will perhaps buy more in a blue environment.

436
00:36:14,300 --> 00:36:17,600
So while the usual goal is to establish a calming environment,

437
00:36:20,100 --> 00:36:21,900
in New York and Connecticut,

438
00:36:22,400 --> 00:36:25,940
Stu Leonard's stores have their own unusual environment.

439
00:36:31,660 --> 00:36:34,720
The audio animatronics, costumed characters,

440
00:36:35,180 --> 00:36:37,120
and general ambience of a county fair

441
00:36:37,121 --> 00:36:40,160
have made these stores a favorite family destination.

442
00:36:40,161 --> 00:36:45,860
The mix of good food and theme park-style fun is paying off.

443
00:36:48,400 --> 00:36:51,460
There are only three Stu Leonard stores so far,

444
00:36:51,880 --> 00:36:55,060
but with revenues hitting $300 million a year,

445
00:36:55,480 --> 00:36:56,760
expansion is in the works.

446
00:36:57,940 --> 00:37:00,680
Standing out from other stores has become very important

447
00:37:00,681 --> 00:37:02,380
in this highly competitive business.

448
00:37:05,660 --> 00:37:08,760
And there is one chain that has become hugely successful

449
00:37:08,761 --> 00:37:14,020
by standing out because of the unique foods it carries, natural and organic.

450
00:37:15,520 --> 00:37:21,020
Over three-quarters of Americans use coupons to save about $3 billion annually.

451
00:37:22,760 --> 00:37:25,580
The supermarket will return on Modern Marvels.

452
00:37:26,940 --> 00:37:30,920
We now return to the supermarket on Modern Marvels.

453
00:37:33,200 --> 00:37:36,980
With a growing percentage of the public interested in eating healthier,

454
00:37:36,981 --> 00:37:40,180
more and more supermarkets are carrying natural foods.

455
00:37:41,380 --> 00:37:43,560
But one large and successful chain

456
00:37:43,561 --> 00:37:45,260
has built its reputation on it.

457
00:37:46,520 --> 00:37:47,260
Whole Foods.

458
00:37:48,320 --> 00:37:50,940
You'll even find a store in the pricey real estate

459
00:37:50,941 --> 00:37:52,460
of Manhattan's Columbus Circle.

460
00:37:56,280 --> 00:37:59,120
Whole Foods, now a Fortune 500 company,

461
00:37:59,720 --> 00:38:02,200
has worked hard to transform a trip to the supermarket

462
00:38:02,201 --> 00:38:03,720
into a favorite destination.

463
00:38:03,721 --> 00:38:09,940
While offering food and other products, its customers feel good about purchasing.

464
00:38:11,720 --> 00:38:17,540
When you walk through the door, it is like a filter, that nothing in our store has artificial colors, flavors, and preservatives.

465
00:38:18,900 --> 00:38:21,800
Whole Foods began in Austin, Texas, back in 1980.

466
00:38:23,980 --> 00:38:26,760
When health food was still considered counterculture.

467
00:38:26,761 --> 00:38:38,394
The history of Whole Foods Market, interestingly enough, was the merging of three small, hippie health food stores into one big, hippie supermarket owned by John

468
00:38:38,395 --> 00:38:41,140
Mackey, Craig Weller, and Mark Skiles.

469
00:38:42,260 --> 00:38:47,580
John's vision from that day and why he was in the business really still permeates the company today.

470
00:38:47,800 --> 00:38:51,700
As CEO, he's mission-driven like he was in the very first store.

471
00:38:51,701 --> 00:39:00,200
Just a few blocks from where the original Whole Foods Market opened more than a quarter century ago are the company's corporate headquarters.

472
00:39:02,340 --> 00:39:05,620
At the base of the office building is the chain's biggest store.

473
00:39:09,420 --> 00:39:16,600
80,000 square feet of specially selected foods presented in an upbeat, state-of-the-art shopping environment.

474
00:39:16,601 --> 00:39:23,440
Their proactive approach to marketing often begins even before entering the store.

475
00:39:25,320 --> 00:39:27,620
I'm roasting Hatch Chili's from Hatch, New Mexico.

476
00:39:28,760 --> 00:39:34,780
And the smell of these roasting peppers is going to waft in through the store, down that escalator, through the parking lot.

477
00:39:35,140 --> 00:39:37,740
And people are going to come across here and say, what is that smell?

478
00:39:38,260 --> 00:39:42,680
That opens the door for me to offer them opportunities to taste new things that they never had before.

479
00:39:42,681 --> 00:39:43,680
Thank you.

480
00:39:43,681 --> 00:39:44,680
You got a little kicker.

481
00:39:44,681 --> 00:39:47,680
And of course we do a lot of tasting in the store.

482
00:39:47,681 --> 00:39:50,680
I can tell you how to go.

483
00:39:50,681 --> 00:39:55,680
Whole Foods has staked its reputation on their commitment to quality natural products.

484
00:39:55,681 --> 00:40:01,740
While the higher costs have led to the nickname Whole Paycheck, the company feels their customer

485
00:40:01,741 --> 00:40:04,840
loyalty is built on the philosophy behind their motto.

486
00:40:04,841 --> 00:40:05,840
Whole Foods.

487
00:40:05,841 --> 00:40:06,840
Whole People.

488
00:40:06,841 --> 00:40:08,840
Whole Planet.

489
00:40:08,841 --> 00:40:14,060
Organic is simply a sustainable farming method that leaves the land as it was.

490
00:40:14,140 --> 00:40:17,240
It doesn't take away from the land that it was grown in.

491
00:40:17,380 --> 00:40:23,180
And because of that, the land will be vital for the next 20, 50, 100 years and beyond.

492
00:40:24,620 --> 00:40:29,400
Whole Foods believes that being concerned about the food supply doesn't have to result in a dreary diet.

493
00:40:30,400 --> 00:40:32,240
A vast selection of wine.

494
00:40:33,340 --> 00:40:36,300
A super chilled room filled with domestic and imported beers.

495
00:40:36,301 --> 00:40:43,620
Exotic cheeses and tantalizing desserts are just a few ways to overindulge in this foodies

496
00:40:43,621 --> 00:40:46,060
paradise.

497
00:40:46,061 --> 00:40:52,120
Whole Foods augments the customer service in its flagship store with a variety of technologies.

498
00:40:52,121 --> 00:40:56,120
There are digital billboards across the front of the store delivering store and community

499
00:40:56,121 --> 00:40:59,180
event information.

500
00:40:59,181 --> 00:41:04,200
Computer stations and multiple touch screens throughout the store offer price checks, product

501
00:41:04,201 --> 00:41:10,560
information, recipes, and store maps. All of these, along with the refrigeration and

502
00:41:10,561 --> 00:41:12,277
lighting, gobble up power.

503
00:41:12,278 --> 00:41:16,240
But Whole Foods has found a way to maintain its eco-friendly

504
00:41:16,241 --> 00:41:18,360
values.

505
00:41:18,361 --> 00:41:24,540
We buy energy efficient lighting for the most part and we have controls on our lighting

506
00:41:24,541 --> 00:41:29,600
system that bring our lights up and down at this store, depending on the sunlight that's

507
00:41:29,601 --> 00:41:34,700
outside to help control energy costs in the store.

508
00:41:34,701 --> 00:41:39,600
And also, Whole Foods Market is the number one purchaser of wind energy in the country,

509
00:41:39,601 --> 00:41:41,100
corporate purchaser of wind energy.

510
00:41:41,101 --> 00:41:45,780
So we're very proud of that.

511
00:41:45,781 --> 00:41:50,760
Whole Foods, like other supermarket chains, has risked billions betting on the right formula

512
00:41:50,761 --> 00:41:53,580
in this highly competitive business.

513
00:41:53,581 --> 00:41:58,380
But oddly, customers have also been rediscovering the simplest and oldest style of shopping

514
00:41:58,381 --> 00:42:01,600
for food.

515
00:42:01,601 --> 00:42:06,600
The street market.

516
00:42:06,601 --> 00:42:11,260
According to the Department of Agriculture, there are currently around 4,800 farmers markets

517
00:42:11,261 --> 00:42:13,140
operating in the United States.

518
00:42:13,141 --> 00:42:18,580
It's interesting to think that the things that were time consuming and a chore and nobody

519
00:42:18,581 --> 00:42:23,980
wanted to do in the 30s, now we've evolved to the point where they're kind of fun and

520
00:42:23,981 --> 00:42:29,440
maybe a little bit of luxury and we will spend some time in these kinds of markets.

521
00:42:31,100 --> 00:42:34,682
But of course there's more to modern life than fresh produce.

522
00:42:34,683 --> 00:42:36,620
So when it comes time to stock up

523
00:42:36,621 --> 00:42:42,260
on everything else, from canned goods to kitty litter, not to mention paper towels, soft drinks

524
00:42:42,261 --> 00:42:47,240
and light bulbs, the odds are you'll head back to the place where you know you'll find a never-ending

525
00:42:47,241 --> 00:42:49,720
Your neighborhood supermarket.

